PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A}

The accredited investor (AI) definition is set out in NI 45-106 (Ontario) and the companion Q&A in Companion Policy 45-106CP. Relevant categories include:

- an individual who, alone or with a spouse, meets the net-income test (> $200 k / > $300 k) or the net-financial-assets test (> $1 M) or the net-assets test (> $5 M);
- a corporation or limited partnership that has net assets of at least $5 M;
- any entity whose owners are all AIs.

Option A describes a limited partnership whose three limited partners are each AIs on an individual basis, but the LP itself is never stated to have net assets ≥ $5 M. Because the entity is a limited partnership, the “all owners are AIs” safe-harbour does not automatically apply; the statute therefore requires the LP itself to satisfy the $5 M asset test. That test is not met on the facts given, so the LP is not an AI.

Options B–E each satisfy at least one of the enumerated tests on their face (joint-income test, net-assets test for the individual or the corporation, or the “all owners are AIs” test). Consequently only A fails to qualify.

