7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {E remains the clearest non-qualifier. Under NI 45-106 s. 1.1 (Ontario, Jan 2021) an entity qualifies only if its most recent financial statements show net assets of at least $5 M or every equity owner is itself an AI. E meets neither: Alex and Bob each fall short on every individual test (income, NFA, net assets), eliminating the look-through route, while the only quantified items produce net assets of $5.2 M ($5.5 M GIC less $300 k liabilities). Because the value of the investment properties is never supplied, the $5 M threshold cannot be confirmed on the facts.  

In contrast, A satisfies the “all owners are AIs” test once the $6 M gifted capital to the GP holding company is recognized (Liam/Ace via NFA, Jack via net assets, holding co via the gifted amount). B meets the joint-spousal income test ($320 k / $350 k combined in each of the two prior years plus current employment). C meets the combined net-assets test (= exactly $5 M). D meets the entity net-assets test (≥ $10 M capital contribution).  

The persistent minority view on B rests on an overly narrow reading of the spousal-aggregation language in the Companion Policy; once the correct per-year combined threshold is applied, only E fails every available pathway.}

